\begin{abstract}
We investigate the ground-state transition in a spin-$1/2$ square-lattice model with nearest-neighbor antiferromagnetic exchange and a four-spin plaquette interaction. By examining magnetic and singlet correlations together with the low-energy excitation spectrum, we characterize the evolution between N\'eel order and a nonmagnetic valence-bond-solid regime. Near the transition, an unusually high density of low-lying singlet and triplet excitations develops, and modes in both spin sectors soften and condense. This simultaneous proliferation of magnetic and nonmagnetic fluctuations is inconsistent with a description based on a single conventional order-parameter mode and points to strongly coupled competing orders. The accessible data exhibit pronounced finite-size effects and do not permit a definitive distinction between a genuinely continuous transition and a weakly first-order transition with a large correlation length. Our results therefore establish the collective singlet--triplet mode condensation as a central feature of the transition while leaving its ultimate order unresolved.
\end{abstract}
